Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: the corner where one rounding ends at a vertex of more than three
edges, one of them a concave sharp edge, is set back along the concave
edges only, and its plate meets its borders G0, so that it stays inside
the body (mitcad#2038).

The bent guide of 1800 to 1803 (GitHub #456): the rounding (0.5) of each
rib's sloped edge, where its end face meets its underside, ends at its foot
on the tube's rim, a vertex of four edges: the rounded edge, the rib's end
on the flat (concave), the flat's rim past the rib (sharp) and the smooth
edge under the rib where its underside goes on into the tube. The end is
PerformMoreThreeCorner's corner of one stripe (from
PerformIntersectionAtEnd: the smooth edge beside the rounded one). It set
back 0.5 along every sharp edge, the concave one too: the plate's borders
left that edge's point up the rib's end face and out along the flat, and a
smooth plate between them turned the concave corner on its air side. All
twelve such corners reached 0.017 to 0.029 out of the body above the flat
past the rib, at every bend radius; MitCad's dressup check warned of the
two whose plate tolerance is the smallest ("the corner of the fillet
reaches 0.02 mm out of the body"), on opening the design at its own radius
of 45 and at 17 and 18.

- PerformMoreThreeCorner, for one stripe (not two tangent stripes, not
  OnSame + OnDiff, no free border, no seam), where a sharp edge at the
  vertex that is neither regular nor tangent to another edge is concave
  (ChFi3d::DefineConnectType): the set-back is taken along the concave
  edges only; every other sharp edge keeps its point at the vertex
  (1801's walk over a vertex is not tried). Such a point is the vertex in
  the DS (as for two tangent stripes) and its edge is not limited. The
  plate's border on the face beyond the concave edge then runs along that
  edge from the vertex, and the plate closes against the faces the rounding
  takes away. Its constraints are all G0: at the vertex the faces meet at
  sharp angles and no tangency holds there (the corner's G1 error was 1.53
  rad before too); held G1 to the rounding and to the faces, the plate's
  fit to its borders came out 0.05 off at the inner rim.
- Elsewhere nothing changes. No catch is added.

On the guide at 45 the twelve corners now reach at most 0.0003 out of the
body (within their plates' tolerances, 0.0011 to 0.0013), and every corner
is valid at bend radii from 16.8 to 50. The rounding takes slightly more
material than before (0.06 mm3 over the twelve corners, about 5e-5 of the
body).

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 1805.

